"""DISPLAY-HINT parsing, validation, rendering and parsing back (RFC 2579 section 3.1).

Used at runtime by the SNMP value conversion (`string-hint`) and by the CLI; it imports
nothing from the MIB compiler.

`parse` compiles a hint into a `DisplayHint`. An integer-format hint is `x`, `o`, `b`, `d` or
`d-N`; an octet-format hint is a sequence of `[*]<length><format>[sep[term]]` specifications,
so it always starts with `*` or a digit. `check` tells whether a hint fits an SMI base type.

The render functions never raise. When a value does not fit the hint they fall back to
lower-case hex pairs joined by `:` (the yang:hex-string form) or to the decimal number, and
the `_ex` variants report that with `ok = False` and a reason. `parse_octets` and `parse_int`
are the inverse, for SET and configuration, and raise `HintError` on text that does not match.
Where rendering is not injective (`1d` renders both 01 02 and 0c as `12`) they return octets
that render to the same text.

Rendering policy (RFC mode, the default), beyond the RFC text:

- `x` renders every application zero-padded to two hex digits per octet consumed (matches
  yang:mac-address and pysnmp for one-octet applications). `d` and `o` render the big-endian
  unsigned number without leading zeros, also beyond 64 bits.
- An application of length 0 consumes no octets and renders nothing (it only produces its
  separator, as in the `0a[2x:...]0a:2d` idiom). When a length-0 specification would have to
  be re-applied to remaining octets (it can never consume them), the value falls back.
- When the specifications are used up, the last one is re-applied including its repeat
  indicator (RFC 2579 section 3.1 "the last octet-format specification is re-interpreted").
- Repetitions stop when the value is exhausted. A separator or terminator is suppressed when
  nothing but other separators and terminators follow it (RFC rule "suppressed if it would
  occur as the last character of the display", applied repeatedly).
- `a` is ASCII per the RFC, but octets >= 0x80 that form valid UTF-8 are accepted and rendered
  as text (devices put UTF-8 into DisplayString). `t` is UTF-8. Octets that are not valid UTF-8
  make the value fall back, except an incomplete character at the end of a `t` application,
  which is discarded as the RFC says. When consecutive `a`/`t` applications are rendered with
  nothing between them (no separator, as with the common `1t` and `1a` hints), a character
  split between them is joined, so `1t` renders UTF-8 text instead of dropping every
  multi-octet character.
- Control characters (including NUL) are kept as they are; escaping them is the caller's job.

`netsnmp=True` reproduces net-snmp 5.9 (`sprint_realloc_octet_string`,
`sprint_realloc_hinted_integer`) for comparing CLI output, verified against libnetsnmp:

- `x` without padding, except a one-octet application below 16 when the specification has no
  separator and is the last one (`0:1a:2b:3c:4d:5e` for `1x:`, `0a0b0c` for `1x`);
- the separator is written whenever octets remain, also before the repeat terminator; the
  terminator is written when octets remain;
- the re-applied last specification does not read a repeat count; a length of 0 counts as 1;
- numbers are C `long`s: `d` of more than 7 octets wraps to a signed 64-bit value, `x` and `o`
  print its 64-bit two's complement;
- `a`/`t` octets are copied as they are; an application containing NUL prints printable
  ASCII, escapes `\\` and `"` and prints other octets as `.`; octets that are not valid UTF-8
  make the value fall back (net-snmp prints them raw);
- integers: `d-N` without a leading zero (`.05`, `-.05`), `x` and `o` of a negative value in
  64-bit two's complement, `b` as exactly 32 binary digits.
- Not reproduced: net-snmp reads past the end of the value when a numeric application has
  fewer octets left than its length (undefined behaviour); here only the remaining octets are
  used.

Known pysnmp 7.1 differences (`TextualConvention.prettyOut`; the comparison vectors are in
test_hint.act): `x` pads to 2 digits per application (`2001:db8:00:...`), `o` pads to 3
digits; the specification list restarts from the beginning instead of re-applying the last
one; `*` is broken (the separator is dropped and the terminator written after every
repetition, and missing octets render as 0); `a` and `t` drop non-ASCII or split characters;
a length-0 specification loops forever; integers: `x` gives `0xff`, `o` `010`, `b` `101B`
(and loops forever on a negative value), `d-N` computes with floats.
"""

_FORMATS = "xdoat"

# The longest OCTET STRING (RFC 2578 section 7.1.2); parse_octets refuses to build a longer one
MAX_OCTETS = 65535

_HEX: list[str] = ["%02x" % i for i in range(256)]

# UTF-8 scan results
_UTF8_OK = 0
_UTF8_INCOMPLETE = 1
_UTF8_INVALID = 2


class HintError(ValueError):
    """A DISPLAY-HINT that does not parse, or text that does not match a hint (`parse_*`)."""
    def __init__(self, msg: str):
        ValueError.__init__(self, msg)


class OctetSpec(object):
    """One octet-format specification: `repeat` (leading `*`), `length` (octets per application,
    may be 0), `fmt` (x, d, o, a or t), `sep` and `term` (one character each, "" when absent;
    `term` only with `repeat` and `sep`)."""
    repeat: bool
    length: int
    fmt: str
    sep: str
    term: str

    def __init__(self, repeat: bool, length: int, fmt: str, sep: str, term: str):
        self.repeat = repeat
        self.length = length
        self.fmt = fmt
        self.sep = sep
        self.term = term

    def __str__(self) -> str:
        return ("*" if self.repeat else "") + str(self.length) + self.fmt + self.sep + self.term

    def __repr__(self) -> str:
        return "OctetSpec(" + self.__str__() + ")"


class DisplayHint(object):
    """A compiled DISPLAY-HINT. `text` is the hint as written. `integer` tells the form: an
    integer-format hint has `int_fmt` (d, x, o or b) and `decimals` (the N of `d-N`, else 0); an
    octet-format hint has `specs`."""
    text: str
    integer: bool
    int_fmt: str
    decimals: int
    specs: list[OctetSpec]

    def __init__(self, text: str, integer: bool, int_fmt: str, decimals: int, specs: list[OctetSpec]):
        self.text = text
        self.integer = integer
        self.int_fmt = int_fmt
        self.decimals = decimals
        self.specs = specs

    def __str__(self) -> str:
        return self.text

    def __repr__(self) -> str:
        return "DisplayHint(" + self.text + ")"


class Rendered(object):
    """A rendering: `text`, and `ok` False when the value did not fit the hint and `text` is
    the fallback (`reason` says why). `dropped` counts the octets of incomplete UTF-8
    characters a `t` application discarded (RFC mode; the text is still `ok`)."""
    text: str
    ok: bool
    reason: str
    dropped: int

    def __init__(self, text: str, ok: bool, reason: str, dropped: int=0):
        self.text = text
        self.ok = ok
        self.reason = reason
        self.dropped = dropped

    def __repr__(self) -> str:
        if self.ok:
            if self.dropped > 0:
                return "Rendered(" + repr(self.text) + ", %d octets dropped)" % self.dropped
            return "Rendered(" + repr(self.text) + ")"
        return "Rendered(" + repr(self.text) + ", fallback: " + self.reason + ")"


# --- parsing -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def _is_digit(c: str) -> bool:
    return c >= "0" and c <= "9"


def _all_digits(s: str) -> bool:
    if s == "":
        return False
    for c in s:
        if not _is_digit(c):
            return False
    return True


def _is_sep(c: str) -> bool:
    """A separator or terminator character: anything but a decimal digit and `*`."""
    return c != "*" and not _is_digit(c)


def _significant(s: str) -> int:
    """The number of digits of s without its leading zeros (s is all digits; "0" counts 1)."""
    k = 0
    while k < len(s) - 1 and s[k] == "0":
        k += 1
    return len(s) - k


def parse(text: str) -> DisplayHint:
    """Compile a DISPLAY-HINT (RFC 2579 section 3.1); raises HintError.

    The text is taken as it is: no white space is stripped (a blank can be a separator)."""
    n = len(text)
    if n == 0:
        raise HintError("empty DISPLAY-HINT")
    c0 = text[0]
    if c0 == "x" or c0 == "o" or c0 == "b":
        if n == 1:
            return DisplayHint(text, True, c0, 0, [])
        raise HintError("integer-format \"" + text + "\": nothing may follow " + c0)
    if c0 == "d":
        if n == 1:
            return DisplayHint(text, True, "d", 0, [])
        if text[1] == "-" and _all_digits(text[2:]):
            if _significant(text[2:]) > 3:
                raise HintError("integer-format \"" + text + "\": too many decimal places")
            return DisplayHint(text, True, "d", int(text[2:]), [])
        raise HintError("integer-format \"" + text + "\": expected d or d-<digits>")
    specs: list[OctetSpec] = []
    i = 0
    while i < n:
        rep = False
        if text[i] == "*":
            rep = True
            i += 1
        j = i
        while i < n and _is_digit(text[i]):
            i += 1
        if i == j:
            raise HintError("octet-format \"" + text + "\": octet length expected at position %d" % j)
        if _significant(text[j:i]) > 6:
            raise HintError("octet-format \"" + text + "\": octet length too large")
        length = int(text[j:i])
        if i >= n:
            raise HintError("octet-format \"" + text + "\": display format expected at the end")
        f = text[i]
        if f not in _FORMATS:
            raise HintError("octet-format \"" + text + "\": bad display format '" + f + "' at position %d" % i)
        i += 1
        sep = ""
        term = ""
        if i < n and _is_sep(text[i]):
            sep = text[i]
            i += 1
            if rep and i < n and _is_sep(text[i]):
                term = text[i]
                i += 1
        specs.append(OctetSpec(rep, length, f, sep, term))
    return DisplayHint(text, False, "", 0, specs)


def try_parse(text: str) -> ?DisplayHint:
    """Like `parse`, but None when the hint does not parse."""
    try:
        return parse(text)
    except HintError:
        return None


def check(h: DisplayHint, base: str, enumerated: bool=False, strict: bool=False) -> str:
    """Why hint `h` does not fit the SMI base type `base`, "" when it fits.

    `base` is a wire type name as in mibmeta's `SyntaxMeta.wire` (Integer32 is taken as
    INTEGER). Nothing fits an enumeration (`enumerated`), BITS, OBJECT IDENTIFIER or IpAddress.
    Otherwise, without `strict`, a hint fits when it can be rendered: an integer-format hint on
    any integer type, an octet-format hint on OCTET STRING and Opaque. `strict` adds the RFC
    2579 section 3.1 prohibitions (Counter32, Counter64) and allows octet-format hints only on OCTET
    STRING."""
    b = "INTEGER" if base == "Integer32" else base
    if b == "BITS" or b == "OBJECT IDENTIFIER" or b == "IpAddress":
        return "RFC 2579 §3.1 forbids a DISPLAY-HINT on " + b
    if enumerated:
        return "RFC 2579 §3.1 forbids a DISPLAY-HINT on an enumeration"
    if h.integer:
        if b == "INTEGER" or b == "Unsigned32" or b == "Gauge32" or b == "TimeTicks":
            return ""
        if b == "Counter32" or b == "Counter64":
            return "RFC 2579 §3.1 forbids a DISPLAY-HINT on " + b if strict else ""
        if b == "OCTET STRING" or b == "Opaque":
            return "integer-format DISPLAY-HINT \"" + h.text + "\" on " + b
        return "no DISPLAY-HINT applies to " + (b if b != "" else "an unknown type")
    if b == "OCTET STRING":
        return ""
    if b == "Opaque":
        return "an octet-format DISPLAY-HINT on Opaque is not RFC 2579" if strict else ""
    if b == "INTEGER" or b == "Unsigned32" or b == "Gauge32" or b == "TimeTicks" or b == "Counter32" or b == "Counter64":
        return "octet-format DISPLAY-HINT \"" + h.text + "\" on " + b
    return "no DISPLAY-HINT applies to " + (b if b != "" else "an unknown type")


# --- helpers ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

def hex_fallback(b: bytes) -> str:
    """The octet fallback: lower-case hex pairs joined by `:` ("" for no octets)."""
    return ":".join([_HEX[c] for c in b])


def _big_digits(v: bigint, base: int) -> str:
    """Digits of a non-negative bigint in base 2, 8, 10 or 16, without leading zeros."""
    if base == 10:
        return str(v)
    if v == bigint(0):
        return "0"
    bb = bigint(base)
    out: list[str] = []
    x = v
    while x > bigint(0):
        d = int(x % bb)
        out.append("0123456789abcdef"[d])
        x = x // bb
    out.reverse()
    return "".join(out)


def _bin_digits(v: int) -> str:
    """Binary digits of a non-negative int, without leading zeros."""
    if v == 0:
        return "0"
    out: list[str] = []
    x = v
    while x > 0:
        out.append("1" if (x & 1) == 1 else "0")
        x = x >> 1
    out.reverse()
    return "".join(out)


def _mag_digits(v: int, f: str) -> str:
    """Digits of |v| for format d, x, o or b (also for the most negative int)."""
    if v < 0:
        if v == -9223372036854775807 - 1:
            base = 10 if f == "d" else 16 if f == "x" else 8 if f == "o" else 2
            return _big_digits(-bigint(v), base)
        return _mag_digits(-v, f)
    if f == "d":
        return str(v)
    if f == "x":
        return "%x" % v
    if f == "o":
        return "%o" % v
    return _bin_digits(v)


def _u64_digits(v: int, f: str) -> str:
    """Digits of v read as an unsigned 64-bit number (C `%lx`, `%lo`)."""
    if v >= 0:
        return _mag_digits(v, f)
    u = bigint(v) + (bigint(1) << 64)
    return _big_digits(u, 16 if f == "x" else 8 if f == "o" else 10)


def _utf8_scan(b: bytes) -> (int, int):
    """(end, status) of the longest valid UTF-8 prefix of b: status _UTF8_OK (all of b),
    _UTF8_INCOMPLETE (b[end:] is a truncated but so far valid character) or _UTF8_INVALID."""
    n = len(b)
    i = 0
    while i < n:
        c = b[i]
        if c < 0x80:
            i += 1
            continue
        need = 0
        lo = 0x80
        hi = 0xbf
        if c >= 0xc2 and c <= 0xdf:
            need = 1
        elif c == 0xe0:
            need = 2
            lo = 0xa0
        elif (c >= 0xe1 and c <= 0xec) or c == 0xee or c == 0xef:
            need = 2
        elif c == 0xed:
            need = 2
            hi = 0x9f
        elif c == 0xf0:
            need = 3
            lo = 0x90
        elif c >= 0xf1 and c <= 0xf3:
            need = 3
        elif c == 0xf4:
            need = 3
            hi = 0x8f
        else:
            return (i, _UTF8_INVALID)
        if i + 1 >= n:
            return (i, _UTF8_INCOMPLETE)
        c1 = b[i + 1]
        if c1 < lo or c1 > hi:
            return (i, _UTF8_INVALID)
        k = 2
        while k <= need:
            if i + k >= n:
                return (i, _UTF8_INCOMPLETE)
            ck = b[i + k]
            if ck < 0x80 or ck > 0xbf:
                return (i, _UTF8_INVALID)
            k += 1
        i += need + 1
    return (n, _UTF8_OK)


def _decode(b: bytes) -> ?str:
    """b as UTF-8 text (b was validated by _utf8_scan); None if the decoder disagrees."""
    try:
        return b.decode()
    except ValueError:
        return None


def _netsnmp_ascii(b: bytes) -> str:
    """net-snmp sprint_realloc_asciistring: printable ASCII, `\\` and `"` escaped, else `.`."""
    out: list[str] = []
    for c in b:
        if c == 0x5c:
            out.append("\\\\")
        elif c == 0x22:
            out.append("\\\"")
        elif c >= 0x20 and c < 0x7f:
            out.append(chr(c))
        else:
            out.append(".")
    return "".join(out)


def _number(b: bytes, s: int, e: int, f: str) -> str:
    """RFC mode: octets b[s:e] as a big-endian unsigned number in format x, d or o."""
    if e <= s:
        return ""
    if f == "x":
        return "".join([_HEX[b[i]] for i in range(s, e)])
    if e - s <= 7:
        v: int = 0
        for i in range(s, e):
            v = v * 256 + b[i]
        return str(v) if f == "d" else "%o" % v
    bv = bigint(0)
    for i in range(s, e):
        bv = bv * bigint(256) + bigint(b[i])
    return _big_digits(bv, 10 if f == "d" else 8)


def _number_netsnmp(b: bytes, s: int, e: int, f: str, width: int, pad_ok: bool) -> str:
    """net-snmp mode: octets b[s:e] as a C long (wrapping), printed with %lx, %ld or %lo."""
    v: int = 0
    for i in range(s, e):
        v = v * 256 + b[i]
    if f == "x":
        if v >= 0 and v < 16 and width == 1 and pad_ok:
            return "0%x" % v
        return _u64_digits(v, "x")
    if f == "d":
        return str(v)
    return _u64_digits(v, "o")


# --- rendering -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

class _Out(object):
    """Rendering state: the pieces, the number of pieces up to the last one with text (the
    separators and terminators after it are suppressed), and an incomplete UTF-8 character
    carried from the previous a/t application."""
    pieces: list[str]
    keep: int
    carry: bytes
    carry_fmt: str
    error: str
    dropped: int

    def __init__(self):
        self.pieces = []
        self.keep = 0
        self.carry = b""
        self.carry_fmt = ""
        self.error = ""
        self.dropped = 0

    def resolve(self, netsnmp: bool) -> None:
        """Something other than an a/t application follows: a carried incomplete character is
        discarded after `t` (RFC; counted in `dropped`), and makes the value fall back
        otherwise."""
        if len(self.carry) > 0:
            if self.carry_fmt == "t" and not netsnmp:
                self.dropped += len(self.carry)
                self.carry = b""
            elif self.error == "":
                self.error = "octets that are not valid UTF-8"

    def text(self, t: str) -> None:
        if t != "":
            self.pieces.append(t)
            self.keep = len(self.pieces)

    def sep(self, c: str, netsnmp: bool) -> None:
        self.resolve(netsnmp)
        self.pieces.append(c)

    def chars(self, data: bytes, f: str, netsnmp: bool) -> None:
        """One a/t application."""
        d = self.carry + data if len(self.carry) > 0 else data
        self.carry = b""
        r = _utf8_scan(d)
        end = r.0
        st = r.1
        if st == _UTF8_INVALID:
            if self.error == "":
                self.error = "octets that are not valid UTF-8"
            return
        t = _decode(d[:end] if end < len(d) else d)
        if t is not None:
            self.text(t)
        elif self.error == "":
            self.error = "octets that are not valid UTF-8"
        if st == _UTF8_INCOMPLETE:
            self.carry = d[end:]
            self.carry_fmt = f

    def result(self) -> str:
        if self.keep < len(self.pieces):
            return "".join(self.pieces[:self.keep])
        return "".join(self.pieces)


def _render_octets(h: DisplayHint, b: bytes, netsnmp: bool) -> (str, str, int):
    """(text, "", octets of incomplete characters dropped) or ("", reason, 0)."""
    if h.integer:
        return ("", "integer-format DISPLAY-HINT \"" + h.text + "\" on an octet string", 0)
    specs = h.specs
    ns = len(specs)
    if ns == 0:
        return ("", "empty DISPLAY-HINT", 0)
    n = len(b)
    o = _Out()
    pos = 0
    si = 0
    while pos < n:
        reuse = si >= ns
        sp = specs[ns - 1] if reuse else specs[si]
        is_last = si >= ns - 1
        si += 1
        width = sp.length
        if netsnmp and width == 0:
            width = 1
        if reuse and width == 0 and not sp.repeat:
            return ("", "the octet length 0 of the last octet-format specification cannot consume the remaining octets", 0)
        count = 1
        if sp.repeat and not (netsnmp and reuse):
            count = b[pos]
            pos += 1
        f = sp.fmt
        r = 0
        while r < count and pos < n:
            e = pos + width
            if e > n:
                e = n
            if f == "a" or f == "t":
                if netsnmp and width > 0 and b.find(b"\x00", pos, e) >= 0:
                    o.resolve(netsnmp)
                    o.text(_netsnmp_ascii(b[pos:e]))
                else:
                    o.chars(b[pos:e], f, netsnmp)
            else:
                o.resolve(netsnmp)
                if netsnmp:
                    o.text(_number_netsnmp(b, pos, e, f, width, sp.sep == "" and is_last))
                else:
                    o.text(_number(b, pos, e, f))
            pos = e
            r += 1
            if sp.sep != "":
                if netsnmp:
                    if pos < n:
                        o.sep(sp.sep, netsnmp)
                elif not (r == count and sp.term != ""):
                    o.sep(sp.sep, netsnmp)
        if sp.term != "":
            if netsnmp:
                if pos < n:
                    o.sep(sp.term, netsnmp)
            else:
                o.sep(sp.term, netsnmp)
        if o.error != "":
            return ("", o.error, 0)
    o.resolve(netsnmp)
    if o.error != "":
        return ("", o.error, 0)
    return (o.result(), "", o.dropped)


def render_octets_ex(h: DisplayHint, b: bytes, netsnmp: bool=False) -> Rendered:
    """Render an OCTET STRING value with an octet-format hint (see the module doc); never raises.
    `netsnmp` selects the net-snmp 5.9 compatible rendering. `Rendered.dropped` counts the
    octets of incomplete characters a `t` application discarded."""
    r = _render_octets(h, b, netsnmp)
    if r.1 == "":
        return Rendered(r.0, True, "", r.2)
    return Rendered(hex_fallback(b), False, r.1)


def render_octets(h: DisplayHint, b: bytes, netsnmp: bool=False) -> str:
    """The text of `render_octets_ex` (the hex fallback when the value does not fit)."""
    r = _render_octets(h, b, netsnmp)
    if r.1 == "":
        return r.0
    return hex_fallback(b)


def _render_int(h: DisplayHint, v: int, netsnmp: bool) -> (str, str):
    if not h.integer:
        return ("", "octet-format DISPLAY-HINT \"" + h.text + "\" on an integer")
    f = h.int_fmt
    if netsnmp:
        if f == "x" or f == "o":
            return (_u64_digits(v, f), "")
        if f == "b":
            out: list[str] = []
            bit = 31
            while bit >= 0:
                out.append("1" if ((v >> bit) & 1) == 1 else "0")
                bit -= 1
            return ("".join(out), "")
        digits = _mag_digits(v, "d")
        k = h.decimals
        if k > 0:
            ln = len(digits)
            if k <= ln:
                digits = digits[:ln - k] + "." + digits[ln - k:]
            else:
                digits = "." + "0" * (k - ln) + digits
        return ("-" + digits if v < 0 else digits, "")
    digits = _mag_digits(v, f)
    k = h.decimals
    if f == "d" and k > 0:
        ln = len(digits)
        if ln <= k:
            digits = "0" * (k - ln + 1) + digits
            ln = k + 1
        digits = digits[:ln - k] + "." + digits[ln - k:]
    return ("-" + digits if v < 0 else digits, "")


def render_int_ex(h: DisplayHint, v: int, netsnmp: bool=False) -> Rendered:
    """Render an integer with an integer-format hint: `d` decimal, `d-N` with N implied decimal
    places (`12.34`, `-0.05`), `x` hex (lower case), `o` octal, `b` binary, a minus sign before
    the digits of a negative value (RFC 2579 section 3.1). Never raises; an octet-format hint gives the
    decimal fallback with `ok = False`."""
    r = _render_int(h, v, netsnmp)
    if r.1 == "":
        return Rendered(r.0, True, "")
    return Rendered(str(v), False, r.1)


def render_int(h: DisplayHint, v: int, netsnmp: bool=False) -> str:
    """The text of `render_int_ex`."""
    r = _render_int(h, v, netsnmp)
    if r.1 == "":
        return r.0
    return str(v)


def format_octets(hint: str, b: bytes, netsnmp: bool=False) -> str:
    """Parse `hint` and render `b`; the hex fallback when the hint does not parse."""
    h = try_parse(hint)
    if h is not None:
        return render_octets(h, b, netsnmp)
    return hex_fallback(b)


def format_int(hint: str, v: int, netsnmp: bool=False) -> str:
    """Parse `hint` and render `v`; the decimal number when the hint does not parse."""
    h = try_parse(hint)
    if h is not None:
        return render_int(h, v, netsnmp)
    return str(v)


# --- parsing values back (SET, configuration) ------------------------------------------------------

def _digit_value(c: str, f: str) -> int:
    """The value of digit c in format f (x, d, o, b), or -1."""
    o = ord(c)
    v = -1
    if o >= 48 and o <= 57:
        v = o - 48
    elif o >= 97 and o <= 102:
        v = o - 87
    elif o >= 65 and o <= 70:
        v = o - 55
    base = 16 if f == "x" else 10 if f == "d" else 8 if f == "o" else 2
    if v >= base:
        return -1
    return v


def _utf8_len(c: str) -> int:
    o = ord(c)
    if o < 0x80:
        return 1
    if o < 0x800:
        return 2
    if o < 0x10000:
        return 3
    return 4


class _In(object):
    """Parsing state over the text."""
    text: str
    pos: int
    out: list[int]

    def __init__(self, text: str):
        self.text = text
        self.pos = 0
        self.out = []

    def more(self) -> bool:
        return self.pos < len(self.text)

    def at(self, c: str) -> bool:
        return c != "" and self.pos < len(self.text) and self.text[self.pos] == c

    def fail(self, msg: str) -> HintError:
        return HintError(msg + " at position %d of \"" % self.pos + self.text + "\"")


def _parse_number(st: _In, sp: OctetSpec, stops: str) -> None:
    """One x/d/o application: digits, then the value as `length` big-endian octets, or as
    fewer octets for a short final `x` application (an even number of digits, fewer than two
    per octet of the length, that ends the text: the renderer pads to 2 digits per octet, so
    only the end of the value gives fewer)."""
    if sp.length == 0:
        # renders nothing, so it reads nothing (its digits belong to the next application)
        return
    t = st.text
    n = len(t)
    f = sp.fmt
    start = st.pos
    limit = n
    stop_chars = stops
    if f == "x" and (sp.sep == "" or _digit_value(sp.sep, "x") >= 0):
        # fixed width (the renderer writes 2 digits per octet) without a separator, or with a
        # separator that is itself a hex digit (`1xa`: unpadded groups would be ambiguous)
        stop_chars = ""
        if start + 2 * sp.length < limit:
            limit = start + 2 * sp.length
    # without a separator, a 0 is an application of its own: the renderer writes no leading
    # zeros, so 0 followed by more digits is two applications (`1d` over 00 39 renders 057)
    zero_app = (f == "d" or f == "o") and sp.sep == "" and start < n and t[start] == "0"
    if zero_app:
        limit = start + 1
    bv = bigint(0)
    base = bigint(16 if f == "x" else 10 if f == "d" else 8)
    # significant digits of the largest value that fits (3 per octet bound d and o): more is an
    # error, and is not accumulated (bigint arithmetic on long input is quadratic)
    maxsig = (2 if f == "x" else 3) * (sp.length if sp.length < MAX_OCTETS else MAX_OCTETS)
    sig = 0
    i = start
    while i < limit:
        c = t[i]
        if c in stop_chars:
            break
        d = _digit_value(c, f)
        if d < 0:
            break
        if sig > 0 or d > 0:
            sig += 1
        if sig <= maxsig:
            bv = bv * base + bigint(d)
        i += 1
    if i == start:
        raise st.fail("digits expected for \"" + str(sp) + "\"")
    if (f == "d" or f == "o") and sp.sep == "" and i < n and not zero_app:
        raise st.fail("ambiguous: \"" + str(sp) + "\" has no separator and more text follows")
    st.pos = i
    if sig > maxsig:
        raise HintError("value " + t[start:i] + " does not fit in %d octets (\"" % sp.length + str(sp) + "\")")
    nb = sp.length
    nd = i - start
    if f == "x" and i == n and nd < 2 * nb and nd % 2 == 0:
        nb = nd // 2
    if len(st.out) + nb > MAX_OCTETS:
        raise st.fail("more than %d octets" % MAX_OCTETS)
    octs: list[int] = [0] * nb
    x = bv
    k = nb - 1
    b256 = bigint(256)
    while k >= 0 and x != bigint(0):
        octs[k] = int(x % b256)
        x = x // b256
        k -= 1
    if x != bigint(0):
        raise HintError("value " + t[start:i] + " does not fit in %d octets (\"" % nb + str(sp) + "\")")
    for o in octs:
        st.out.append(o)


def _parse_chars(st: _In, sp: OctetSpec, join: bool) -> None:
    """One a/t application: the characters of `length` octets, or of fewer at the end of the
    text. The renderer only ends an application early at the end of the value, so separator
    and terminator characters before the length is reached are data. A character that would
    cross the length is left to the next application, except when it is the first and `join`
    (the next applications are of the same specification with nothing between them, so the
    renderer joins a character split between them); otherwise it does not fit."""
    t = st.text
    n = len(t)
    used = 0
    i = st.pos
    while i < n and used < sp.length:
        ln = _utf8_len(t[i])
        if used + ln > sp.length and (used > 0 or not join):
            break
        used += ln
        i += 1
    if i == st.pos and i < n and sp.length > 0:
        raise st.fail("a character does not fit in \"" + str(sp) + "\"")
    if i > st.pos:
        for x in t[st.pos:i].encode():
            st.out.append(x)
    st.pos = i


def _parse_app(st: _In, sp: OctetSpec, stops: str, join: bool) -> None:
    if sp.fmt == "a" or sp.fmt == "t":
        _parse_chars(st, sp, join)
    else:
        _parse_number(st, sp, stops)


def parse_octets(h: DisplayHint, text: str) -> bytes:
    """The octets whose rendering (RFC mode) is `text`; raises HintError.

    Lenient where the rendering is not: hex digits in either case, leading zeros, and unpadded
    hex groups when the separator is not itself a hex digit (`0:1a:2b:3c:4d:5e` for `1x:`).
    Text that only a short final application can produce (`2x` over three octets renders
    `000102`) parses to the shorter value. A repeat specification needs a terminator unless it
    is the last one. `d` and `o` without a separator are only accepted at the end of the text.
    More than MAX_OCTETS octets are refused."""
    if h.integer:
        raise HintError("integer-format DISPLAY-HINT \"" + h.text + "\" on an octet string")
    specs = h.specs
    ns = len(specs)
    st = _In(text)
    si = 0
    while st.more():
        reuse = si >= ns
        sp = specs[ns - 1] if reuse else specs[si]
        last = si >= ns - 1
        si += 1
        if sp.repeat and (sp.fmt == "a" or sp.fmt == "t") and sp.sep == "":
            # without a separator (so without a terminator) only as the last specification:
            # the renderer joins characters across its applications and repeat counts, so the
            # rest of the text is cut into groups of 255 applications of `length` octets
            if not last or sp.length == 0:
                raise st.fail("ambiguous: repeat \"" + str(sp) + "\" " + ("has no terminator" if not last else "consumes no octets"))
            data = st.text[st.pos:].encode()
            st.pos = len(st.text)
            group = 255 * sp.length
            k = 0
            while k < len(data) and len(st.out) <= MAX_OCTETS:
                e = k + group if k + group < len(data) else len(data)
                st.out.append((e - k + sp.length - 1) // sp.length)
                for o in data[k:e]:
                    st.out.append(o)
                k = e
        elif sp.repeat:
            if sp.term == "" and not last:
                raise st.fail("ambiguous: repeat \"" + str(sp) + "\" has no terminator")
            stops = sp.sep + sp.term
            cpos = len(st.out)
            st.out.append(0)
            count = 0
            # the renderer drops the separator before the terminator, and a terminator that
            # would be the last character, so after an a/t separator, and at the end of the
            # text, an a/t character is data even when it is the terminator character
            chars = sp.fmt == "a" or sp.fmt == "t"
            after_sep = False
            while st.more() and ((chars and (after_sep or st.pos == len(st.text) - 1)) or not st.at(sp.term)):
                if count == 255:
                    if sp.term != "":
                        raise st.fail("more than 255 repetitions")
                    break
                before = st.pos
                _parse_app(st, sp, stops, False)
                if st.pos == before:
                    raise st.fail("cannot parse \"" + str(sp) + "\"")
                count += 1
                after_sep = False
                if st.at(sp.sep):
                    st.pos += 1
                    after_sep = True
                elif st.more() and not st.at(sp.term) and sp.sep != "":
                    raise st.fail("separator '" + sp.sep + "' expected")
            st.out[cpos] = count
            if st.at(sp.term):
                st.pos += 1
        else:
            if reuse and sp.length == 0:
                raise st.fail("the last octet-format \"" + str(sp) + "\" consumes no octets")
            _parse_app(st, sp, sp.sep, sp.sep == "" and last)
            if st.more() and sp.sep != "":
                if not st.at(sp.sep):
                    raise st.fail("separator '" + sp.sep + "' expected")
                st.pos += 1
        if len(st.out) > MAX_OCTETS:
            raise st.fail("more than %d octets" % MAX_OCTETS)
    return bytes(st.out)


def parse_int(h: DisplayHint, text: str) -> int:
    """The integer whose rendering (RFC mode) is `text`; raises HintError.

    `d-N` accepts up to N decimal places (`12.3` is 1230 for `d-2`, `.05` is 5); `x` accepts an
    optional `0x` prefix and either case; a leading `-` is accepted for every format."""
    if not h.integer:
        raise HintError("octet-format DISPLAY-HINT \"" + h.text + "\" on an integer")
    t = text.strip()
    neg = False
    if t.startswith("-"):
        neg = True
        t = t[1:]
    f = h.int_fmt
    if f == "x" and (t.startswith("0x") or t.startswith("0X")):
        t = t[2:]
    k = h.decimals
    frac = ""
    if f == "d" and "." in t:
        if k == 0:
            raise HintError("\"" + text + "\": no decimal places in \"" + h.text + "\"")
        p = t.find(".")
        frac = t[p + 1:]
        t = t[:p]
        if len(frac) > k:
            raise HintError("\"" + text + "\": more than %d decimal places" % k)
        if t == "" and frac == "":
            raise HintError("\"" + text + "\": digits expected")
    elif t == "":
        raise HintError("\"" + text + "\": digits expected")
    if f == "d":
        t = t + frac + "0" * (k - len(frac))
    bv = bigint(0)
    base = bigint(16 if f == "x" else 10 if f == "d" else 8 if f == "o" else 2)
    for c in t:
        d = _digit_value(c, f)
        if d < 0:
            raise HintError("\"" + text + "\": bad digit '" + c + "' for \"" + h.text + "\"")
        bv = bv * base + bigint(d)
    if neg:
        bv = -bv
    if bv > bigint(9223372036854775807) or bv < -bigint(9223372036854775807) - bigint(1):
        raise HintError("\"" + text + "\": out of range")
    return int(bv)
